WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.380
Type safety is a security design tool.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:12.520
The more accurately an API describes its domain through types, the less it depends on callers remembering which string, integer, flag, or sentinel value means what.

3
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:17.120
Strong domain types can separate concepts that would otherwise look identical.

4
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:27.060
A user identifier, tenant identifier, byte count, timeout, permission set, and record version may all be integers or strings internally, but they do not represent the same idea.

5
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:31.740
Scoped enums and explicit constructors reduce accidental conversions.

6
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.040
They make call sites easier to read and prevent unreviewed combinations of loosely typed flags.

7
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.760
References can communicate required, non-null objects.

8
00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:47.420
Pointers can still be appropriate for optional or borrowed access, but the API should make that meaning clear.

9
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.680
Optional values should be modeled explicitly with std::optional or a similarly clear pattern.

10
00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:57.480
Return values should force important outcomes into view.

11
00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:09.340
If an operation can fail, return a result type, expected-like type, status object, or exception policy that callers cannot reasonably ignore in security-sensitive code.

12
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.780
Ambiguous sentinel values are risky.

13
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:21.000
A negative number, empty string, null pointer, or magic value may be confused with a real result unless the contract is obvious and consistently checked.

14
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.000
Expressive types reduce review burden.

15
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:33.300
Reviewers can focus on whether the right authority, object, or validated value reaches the boundary instead of decoding a long list of primitive parameters.

16
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.540
Const correctness contributes to the same goal.

17
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:44.880
A const view or reference communicates that the callee should not mutate caller-owned state, while a mutable reference signals that change is part of the contract.

18
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.120
Return types are also part of the interface design.

19
00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:57.980
A result object can carry success, failure, diagnostic category, and output without relying on out-parameters or loosely connected status flags.

20
00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:03.300
The strongest APIs make accidental misuse look awkward at the call site.
